snippet.textDisplay:
@nuriddinnuriddin-q7n The US and its allies have purchased about 900 THAAD interceptors in the past 18 years. Last year and this year, US consumed more than half of the interceptors in the war with Iran. Guess how many THAAD interceptors have been purchased in 2025? Twelve. Now 3/4 of the over 2300 stored JASSAMs is already used in Iran. And for PAC3, the US has even requested missile buybacks from its allies, even Ukraine. At this point, the inventory and production capacity of US military industry are not enough to sustain even a regional war with Iran. No matter how many bases you have, what's the point if u don't have ammunition? 
Not to mention that the current US shipbuilding industry is less than 1/200 of China‘s’. It took US 6 years and $800 million to repair USS Boise, a SSN, but this year it announced a failure; the US total production capacity of all fighter jets each year is not as good as the sum of the production capacity of China's J-20s and J-16s. At the same time, the F-35 Block4 has also encountered problems such as delayed upgrades and insufficient radar production capacity; the annual flight training hours of USAF pilots are only half of Chinese pilots', and the US Navy faces a serious problem of  technical personnel shortage. And about those army soldiers who only have elementary school math and writing skills, are they competitive compared to the PLA, which is increasingly composed of college students?
War will not guarantee victory just because you have multiple bases and allies. When your industrial base, strategic planning, institutional management, and personnel quality do not meet the needs of modern warfare, your strength is just window paper waiting to be pierced, and Iran and Russia have already opened two holes on it.

